// batch y: ayacucho, junin, nsamankow, passodorosario, alamo, zhenjiang
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "húsares": "Light cavalry of Hungarian origin, with showy uniforms and a saber in hand. In the 19th century, many armies had hussar regiments. Bolívar gave one of them the name Hussars of Junín.",
  "sabre": "A sword with a curved, single-edged blade, made for striking downward from horseback.",
  "cavalaria": "The soldiers who fight on horseback. They are fast at attacking and pursuing, but of little use on marshy or broken ground.",
  "barranco": "A deep ditch cut by water, with steep banks. It slows the march of troops, who can only cross at a few points.",
  "coxilha": "A long, gentle hill, typical of the plains of Rio Grande do Sul and Uruguay.",
  "banda oriental": "The old name for present-day Uruguay: the eastern bank of the Uruguay River. When it was part of Brazil, from 1821 to 1828, it was called the Cisplatine Province.",
  "guerra da cisplatina": "The war between the Empire of Brazil and the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata (the Argentina of the time), from 1825 to 1828, over possession of the Banda Oriental.",
  "axantes": "An Akan people from the interior of present-day Ghana, who built a powerful empire in the 18th century, with its capital at Kumasi. Also spelled Ashanti. Their king sat on a golden throne, the Golden Stool.",
  "fante": "A people of the coast of present-day Ghana, neighbors and rivals of the Asante. Through trade, they allied with the British to protect themselves from the Asante.",
  "foguete congreve": "A war rocket invented by the Englishman William Congreve, with an explosive or incendiary charge at the tip. It was not very accurate, but the noise and the trail of fire frightened men and horses.",
  "sem quartel": "An order to accept no surrender and to spare no one who gives up. 'Giving quarter' meant sparing a defeated enemy.",
  "missão": "A complex of buildings founded by Spanish religious orders to convert and gather Indigenous peoples. The walls were thick, and many missions ended up serving as forts.",
  "navio a vapor": "A ship driven by a steam engine, with paddle wheels or a propeller. It could go up a river against the current and the wind, which a sailing ship could not.",
  "guerra do ópio": "The war between Great Britain and China (1839–1842), which began when the Chinese authorities confiscated and destroyed opium sold by British merchants in Canton.",
  "grande canal": "The longest canal in the world, linking southern China to Beijing. Up it traveled the rice and the grain taxes that fed the capital.",
  "tratado de nanquim": "The treaty of 29 August 1842 that ended the Opium War. China handed over Hong Kong, opened five ports to British trade and paid a large indemnity."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  ayacucho: {
    war: "South American wars of independence (1810–1826): the final campaign in Peru",
    hook: "On a plateau 3,400 meters high, in a few hours, the last significant Spanish army in South America fell.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Peru was the heart of the Spanish empire in South America: the seat of the viceroyalty, with the silver mines and a wealthy elite that, in large part, preferred to stay with the king. That is why it was the last place to be liberated. The armies of San Martín, from the south, and of Bolívar, from the north, only reached it after liberating Argentina, Chile, Colombia and Venezuela.",
        "A European event helped the patriots. In 1820, a liberal revolt in Spain, led by Rafael Riego, kept the king from sending reinforcements to America. The royalists in Peru were left on their own. In addition, in January 1821 officers overthrew Viceroy Pezuela in a coup at Aznapuquio, and José de la Serna took charge. He abandoned Lima, which was blockaded, and set up his capital in Cusco, high in the Andes.",
        { h: "The road to Ayacucho" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1823", "The royalists win at Torata, Moquegua and Zepita and retake Arequipa. The patriots seem lost."],
          ["February 1824", "Nearly 2,000 patriot soldiers, among them Argentines, mutiny at Callao and go over to the king. In Upper Peru, the royalist general Olañeta rebels against La Serna."],
          ["6 August 1824", "Bolívar defeats the royalist cavalry at Junín."],
          ["7 October 1824", "Bolívar hands Sucre command of the southern army, near Cusco."],
          ["3 December 1824", "At Corpahuaico, the royalists attack the patriots and inflict more than 500 casualties, besides the loss of ammunition and artillery."],
          ["9 December 1824", "Battle of Ayacucho, on the Pampa de la Quinua."]
        ]},
        "Olañeta's revolt was the greatest gift the patriots received. Olañeta was an absolutist and did not accept the viceroy, who had liberal ideas. La Serna sent General Jerónimo Valdés, with about 5,000 men, to Upper Peru to put an end to him. The fighting among royalists, which dragged on until August, left both sides weaker and split the army that was to defend the viceroyalty.",
        "They say that when a minister asked Bolívar what to do after the Callao mutiny, he answered with a single word: 'Triumph'."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Antonio José de Sucre, 29 years old" },
        "Born in Cumaná, Venezuela, he was Bolívar's most trusted general and had already won at Pichincha, in 1822. He was more methodical than brilliant. At Corpahuaico he lost more than 500 men, ammunition and artillery, but kept the army in order and fell back to positions where he could defend himself, among quinoa fields. He knew the royalists had food for less than five days, and that time was on his side.",
        { h: "José de la Serna, 54 years old" },
        "Born in Jerez de la Frontera, from a military family, he had fought the French in Spain and been imprisoned in France, from where he escaped in 1812. He had been viceroy since the coup of 1821. By 1824 he had reconquered almost all of Peru, but the quarrel with Olañeta was draining his army. His chief of staff was General José de Canterac.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "José María Córdova, 25 years old, commanded the patriot First Division and is remembered for the order he is said to have given before attacking: 'Arms at will, straight ahead, at the pace of conquerors'. Jacinto Lara commanded the reserve, José de La Mar another division, and William Miller the cavalry. On the royalist side, Valdés commanded the vanguard, Juan Antonio Monet and Alejandro González Villalobos two divisions, José Carratalá the reserve, and Valentín Ferraz the cavalry."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Numbers vary widely by source, and Sucre's own report relied on a list captured from the Spanish. The patriot divisions total about 5,800 men, with Córdova (2,300), La Mar (1,580), Lara (1,700) and a cavalry of fewer than 900, with one or two cannons. The royalists total about 6,900, with Valdés (2,006), Monet (2,000), Villalobos (1,700), Carratalá (1,200) and a cavalry of about 1,000, with 11 to 14 cannons. Higher estimates reach 8,500 patriots and 9,310 royalists, which were probably the size of the armies at the start of the campaign, before losses and desertions.",
        { note: "There is also a disagreement over the order of battle: Sucre's report and Miller's memoirs do not describe in the same way where each regiment stood, such as the Hussars of Junín and the Grenadiers of Buenos Aires. The map below shows only the general layout." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Pampa de la Quinua is a plateau about 3,400 meters high, some 37 kilometers from the city of Ayacucho. The royalists were on top of Condorcunca, a hill with a good view. Between the two armies lay a [[gully|barranco]] and an open slope, which the troops would have to descend and cross under fire.",
        { h: "The night before" },
        "According to popular tradition, told by Ricardo Palma in his Peruvian Traditions, officers from both sides met on neutral ground the evening before and shared bread, cheese and raw cane sugar before going back to fighting each other. Sucre told the troops that the fate of South America depended on the day's effort and ended with 'Long live the Liberator!'.",
        { h: "The battle in six moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the royalist plan, by Canterac: Valdés would cross the Pampas River to hit the patriot left on the flank, while the bulk of the army came down the hill to attack the center. It was a bold plan that needed everything to go right at the same time.",
        "Second, the descent. The royalist battalions came down the slope without protecting their flanks, and the artillery came down with them, unprotected. Sucre saw the risk: the columns were separated by the terrain and none could help another. The patriot [[grapeshot|metralha]] punished the descent.",
        "Third, Córdova's attack. His division advanced with Miller's cavalry, captured the royalist artillery and fell on the disorganized columns. Colonel Joaquín Rubín de Celis, who was guarding the cannons, advanced carelessly, was cut off and died along with most of his unit.",
        "Fourth, Monet's counterattack. He crossed the gully with the First Division, but was surrounded by Córdova's advance. Monet came out wounded, and three of his commanders died. Ferraz's cavalry also charged, but met infantry fire and confusion in its own ranks, and fell back with heavy losses.",
        "Fifth, the royalist right. At the other end, the divisions of La Mar and Lara held off Valdés's attack on an isolated house. With reinforcements and the help of Córdova's victorious division, the patriots counterattacked.",
        "Sixth, the reserve. Canterac led the reserve across the plain, but the Gerona battalions, made up of recruits after the quarrel with Olañeta, scattered quickly, and the Fernando VII battalion resisted little and followed them. Around one in the afternoon, Viceroy La Serna was wounded and a prisoner. Valdés fell back with what was left to a hill at the rear, and about 200 horsemen gathered around Canterac. According to the sources, fleeing soldiers shot some of their own officers."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to Sucre's report, the patriots lost 370 dead and 609 wounded, and the royalists about 1,800 dead, 700 wounded and between 2,000 and 3,000 prisoners. These figures are disputed: some say Sucre understated his own casualties.",
        "With the viceroy wounded and captured, Canterac negotiated on behalf of the army. The capitulation was signed on the battlefield itself, on 9 December, by Canterac and Sucre, with an addendum the next day. In the summaries we read, the royalists ended the fighting, the garrisons of Cusco and Arequipa were handed over and the Peruvian republic took on the payment of debts to countries that had sent troops. We have not read the full text of the capitulation.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Ayacucho ended Spanish rule on the South American mainland, after some 300 years. The fortress of Real Felipe, at Callao, commanded by José Ramón Rodil, held out and only surrendered on 22 January 1826, as did the island of Chiloé, in Chile, also in 1826. In April 1825, Olañeta died at Tumusla, and Valdés surrendered. On 6 August 1825, the anniversary of Junín, Upper Peru declared independence and took the name Bolivia, in honor of Bolívar. The first president was Sucre, because Bolívar turned the office down.",
        "Peru gave Sucre the title of Grand Marshal of Ayacucho. Bolívar praised him in writing.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The agreed-defeat thesis: the Spanish historian Juan Carlos Losada argues that the outcome may have been arranged beforehand, with Monet playing a central role. It would have been an agreement among liberal royalist officers, who preferred a patriot victory to an absolutist Spain. It is only a hypothesis and has no documentary proof." },
        { note: "The name: the battle is also called the Battle of La Quinua, after the pampa where it was fought, some 37 kilometers from Ayacucho." },
        { note: "The numbers: the gap between the estimates, from 5,780 to 8,500 patriots and from 6,906 to 9,310 royalists, shows how imprecise the accounts of the time were. Sucre also inflated the size of the royalists, and his report understated patriot losses." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  junin: {
    war: "South American wars of independence: the Peru campaign (1823–1824)",
    hook: "A battle in which no one fired a shot, more than 4,000 meters above sea level, and which changed the course of the war.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the start of 1824, the patriot cause in Peru was on the ground. In February, nearly 2,000 soldiers, among them Argentines, mutinied at Callao and went over to the royalists, who retook Lima. Bolívar, president of Gran Colombia, had come at the invitation of the Peruvian Congress and was in Trujillo, in the north.",
        "The opportunity came from the other side. The royalists were divided by the revolt of General Olañeta in Upper Peru, and a good part of their troops was sent there. Bolívar climbed the Andes and advanced inland with some 12,000 men, including infantry, cavalry and guerrillas, to isolate the army of General José de Canterac.",
        { timeline: [
          ["February 1824", "Callao mutiny and loss of Lima."],
          ["Mid-1824", "Bolívar climbs the Andes toward central Peru."],
          ["6 August 1824", "The cavalries meet on the Pampa de Junín."],
          ["9 December 1824", "Four months later, at Ayacucho, the royalist army falls."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Simón Bolívar, 41 years old" },
        "A wealthy Venezuelan, the Liberator had already liberated Venezuela and Colombia. He had weathered the Callao mutiny without giving up. At Junín, he watched the start of the fight from a hill and, when he saw the clash going badly, he fell back to hurry up the infantry instead of going to the front.",
        { h: "José de Canterac" },
        "A royalist general and right-hand man of Viceroy La Serna, he commanded the northern army. He had about 1,300 horsemen at Junín, and cavalry was the dominant arm of the royalist army.",
        { h: "The patriot cavalry officers" },
        "The Argentine general Mariano Necochea commanded the Grenadiers of Colombia, alongside the German major Otto Philipp Braun. The Englishman William Miller commanded the Hussars of Peru, with about 250 men. Colonel Silva commanded the Hussars of Colombia, and Lieutenant Colonel Isidoro Suárez the first squadron of the Hussars of Peru, which played the decisive role."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Only the cavalry was on the field: about 1,000 patriots, among Grenadiers of Colombia, Hussars of Peru, Hussars of Colombia and Grenadiers of the Andes, against about 1,300 royalists. The patriot infantry was still far away. Some accounts give much larger numbers for both armies, which seem to refer to the total forces of the campaign, not to those who fought that day.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Pampa de Junín lies on the Bombón plateau, at about 4,100 meters of altitude, near Lake Junín. The ground is marshy, with a narrow passage through which the patriot cavalry had to advance. At that altitude, the air is thin and the cold is biting, and horses and men tire quickly.",
        { h: "The battle in six moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the patriot cavalry crossed the narrow passage and began to form up on the plain. The royalists took advantage of the most vulnerable moment and charged it before it was lined up, pushing it back in confusion.",
        "Second, Necochea's Grenadiers held the first charge. Necochea was wounded several times, according to the official report, and captured.",
        "Third, Miller was ordered to go around the royalist right, but the Spanish charge forced him to attack head-on. Only part of the Grenadiers of Colombia, under Braun's command, managed to break through the enemy lines.",
        "Fourth, Bolívar, who was watching from a hill, fell back to bring up the infantry faster.",
        "Fifth, the surprise. Suárez's squadron, hidden behind a hill, received a message from Major José Andrés Rázuri with a supposed order from General La Mar to attack. Suárez charged the royalist flank and rear.",
        "Sixth, the turnaround. Miller returned to the fight, Silva regrouped the Hussars of Colombia, and the royalist cavalry, struck from behind, broke and fled. In about 45 minutes it was all over. The fighting was done only with lances and [[sabers|sabre]]. Not a single firearm was discharged, which is why the battle is called the 'silent battle'. The patriot infantry arrived after the combat had already ended.",
        { note: "Rázuri's order: the message carried an order attributed to La Mar that, according to Wikipedia, was false. It is said that La Mar later told Rázuri he deserved to be shot, but that the day's victory was his. The sources we read do not say whether it was a deliberate trick or a mistake." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Losses were small: about 150 patriots, according to most accounts, and 250 to 254 royalists, plus some 80 prisoners. The patriots captured more than 400 horses. Numbers vary by source, and some even swap the death totals of the two sides. The official patriot report gives 145 casualties.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first patriot victory of this campaign and it destroyed the royalists' best weapon, their cavalry. Canterac withdrew in disorder to Cusco, with heavy losses of men and equipment, and desertions rose. The [[hussars|húsares]] of Peru received from Bolívar the name Hussars of Junín. Viceroy La Serna took command to raise morale. Four months later, at Ayacucho, the royalists were defeated.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The cold: according to Miller, the following night was so freezing that it killed almost all the wounded on both sides." },
        { note: "Among the patriot dead was Commander Carlos Sowersby, a veteran of Napoleon's Grande Armée." },
        { note: "The battle appears in the sixth stanza of Colombia's national anthem and in poems by José Joaquín de Olmedo and Jorge Luis Borges." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  nsamankow: {
    war: "First Anglo-Asante War (1823–1831)",
    hook: "The soldiers opened the ammunition boxes and found macaroni.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the interior of present-day Ghana, the [[Asante|axantes]] Empire was the strongest state in the region, with its capital at Kumasi and an enormous military tradition. On the coast, the [[Fante|fante]] lived from trade with the Europeans and suffered Asante attacks. In 1821, the British government took over the forts on the Gold Coast and placed them under the governor of Sierra Leone, Charles MacCarthy.",
        "MacCarthy was Irish by birth, the son of a French father, and had already been governor of Senegal and of Sierra Leone. In 1823, after quarrels between the Fante and the Asante, he declared war on the Asante king. His plan was to gather four different columns to march together against the enemy and crush him with superior numbers.",
        { timeline: [
          ["1821", "The British take over the Gold Coast forts."],
          ["Late 1823", "MacCarthy declares war on the Asante king."],
          ["20 January 1824", "MacCarthy's column camps near a tributary of the Pra River, without joining the others."],
          ["21 January 1824", "Battle of Nsamankow."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Charles MacCarthy, 59 years old" },
        "Born in Ireland in 1764, he served in the French, Dutch and British armies. He was knighted in 1820.",
        { h: "The Asante side" },
        "King Osei Bonsu died between the end of 1823 and January 1824, and the sources disagree on the exact date. He was succeeded by his brother, Osei Yaw Akoto, who favored war. Wikipedia lists Amankwatia as the Asante commander at Nsamankow, but there are no details about him in the sources we read."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "With MacCarthy there were some 80 regular soldiers of the Royal African Corps, 170 [[militiamen|milícia]] from Cape Coast and 240 Fante allies, about 500 in all. The Asante were, according to the sources, more than 10,000. The other three British columns, with a few hundred regulars and several thousand local allies, were elsewhere.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The encounter took place near a stream, a tributary of the Pra River, about 18 meters wide, which separated the two sides. It is the Bonsaso region, and so the battle is sometimes also called by that name.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Around two in the afternoon, MacCarthy met the Asante. Hoping that chiefs unhappy with the Asante would come over to his side, he had the band play the British anthem. In response, the Asante advanced beating war drums, and the hope was gone.",
        "The fight turned into an exchange of fire across the stream. The Asante felled trees to make bridges, and the British shot at those crossing them, exposed on the logs. But ammunition was short: the carriers who were bringing almost all the powder and bullets ran away when they heard the shots. Four boxes arrived, and only the first had bullets. The other three, according to the accounts, were full of macaroni, by mistake of the man in charge of supplies.",
        "Around four in the afternoon, the ammunition ran out, and the Asante crossed the stream. Most of the Fante fled. The British who stayed were overwhelmed in hand-to-hand fighting. Only about 20 escaped."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Wikipedia gives 187 British dead and 92 wounded, plus an unknown number of prisoners. The number of Asante dead is not known.",
        "MacCarthy was wounded while retreating with Ensign Wetherell and the colonial secretary, Williams. According to one account, he killed himself to avoid capture; according to another, he was killed by a second shot. Wetherell died defending his body, and Williams was captured and later told of being held in a hut with the severed heads of the two.",
        "MacCarthy's head was taken to Kumasi. According to Britain's National Army Museum, the skull was used as a cup for the king, with a gold rim. It was recovered in 1829 and buried in Dartmouth, England.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The war went on. On 7 August 1826, a British and allied force defeated the Asante at Dodowa, near Accra, using [[Congreve rockets|foguete congreve]]. According to Wikipedia, Osei Yaw Akoto wanted to punish the Ga-Adangbe, who had helped the Fante at Nsamankow.",
        "The Asante fought four more wars against the British. The last, the War of the Golden Stool, was in 1900.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date and the numbers: some sources give 22 January, and casualty figures vary. The story of the heads kept as trophies depends on Williams's account and has no firm source." },
        { note: "In 1874, in the Third Asante War, the British recovered in Kumasi silver forks looted from MacCarthy's baggage in 1824. One of them is today in the National Army Museum, in London." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  passodorosario: {
    war: "Cisplatine War (1825–1828)",
    hook: "Six hours of cavalry against infantry squares, and to this day each country tells the result its own way.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The [[Banda Oriental|banda oriental]], present-day Uruguay, was annexed by Brazil in 1821 and became the Cisplatine Province. In 1825, a group of rebels, the Thirty-Three Orientals, led by Juan Antonio Lavalleja, rose up and declared union with the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata, the future Argentina. Brazil declared war in December 1825 and blockaded the Río de la Plata estuary.",
        "At the end of 1826, the Argentine general Carlos María de Alvear gathered an army at Durazno with the goal of invading Rio Grande do Sul and annexing it to his country. Emperor Pedro I sent reinforcements, and the Marquis of Barbacena took over the Imperial Army of the South. Fighting at Bacacay and Ombú had weakened the Brazilian cavalry. Alvear drew the bulk of the Brazilians to the Santa Maria River.",
        { timeline: [
          ["1821", "The Banda Oriental is annexed to Brazil."],
          ["1825", "The Thirty-Three Orientals rebel, and Brazil declares war in December."],
          ["Late 1826", "Alvear gathers his army at Durazno and marches north."],
          ["20 February 1827", "Battle of Passo do Rosário (Ituzaingó)."],
          ["April 1827", "The Brazilians defeat the Argentine navy at Monte Santiago."],
          ["1828", "The peace convention creates independent Uruguay."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Carlos María de Alvear" },
        "An Argentine general, he commanded the army of the Río de la Plata. His plan was to invade Rio Grande do Sul, and his critics, among them General José María Paz and Tomás de Iriarte, accused him of not having pursued the Brazilians after the battle.",
        { h: "The Marquis of Barbacena" },
        "Commander of the Brazilian army. He was pressed to attack because local recruits were deserting. His cavalry was weaker than the enemy's.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Argentine-Oriental side, Lavalleja commanded the center corps, the Frenchman Carlos Brandsen, a veteran of Napoleon's wars, commanded the infantry, and Generals Juan Lavalle and José María Paz the cavalry. On the Brazilian side, Marshal José de Abreu, Baron of Cerro Largo, commanded volunteers on the left wing. Bento Manuel Ribeiro had been sent to watch the enemy's march and did not return in time for the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Numbers vary. English Wikipedia gives about 7,700 men for the Argentine-Oriental side and 6,300 for the Brazilian, and the Portuguese one speaks of about 8,000 against 6,000. The Argentine-Oriental side had a clear advantage in cavalry.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Argentine-Oriental army occupied two parallel [[ridges|coxilha]] to the west, closer to the Passo do Rosário, on the Santa Maria River. The Brazilians stayed on the east ridge. Between them lay a sanga, a ditch that could only be crossed at a few points. Argentines and Uruguayans call the battle Ituzaingó, the name of a nearby stream that appears on a map. The Brazilians use the name of the river crossing.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "The accounts disagree on several points. One version says the Brazilian troops reached the battle zone around six in the morning, after a forced march, and had to fight immediately. Another, from a Spanish source, says the Brazilians were taken by surprise because they believed the enemy had already crossed the swollen river, while the allies made a night countermarch and hid their cavalry.",
        "The artillery opened fire around 7:30. Barbacena sent most of the infantry against Lavalleja's corps, in the center, and his cavalry brigades made early gains on the wings. Brandsen's infantry counterattacked and was repelled by the Brazilian [[squares|quadrado]]. Brandsen died.",
        "Around noon, the enemy cavalry attacked the Brazilian left, and the volunteers of the Baron of Cerro Largo fell apart. The baron was mortally wounded. The fugitives and the enemy horsemen overran the baggage and the artillery train. There were further charges, including one by Lavalle. Some cannons were lost and retaken, but the squares of the Brazilian infantry stayed firm and repelled the attacks.",
        "Around one in the afternoon, Barbacena ordered the first division back to its starting position and, at two, ordered the retreat sounded. Alvear had the grass around the field set on fire. The fighting lasted about six hours, according to most sources. The Portuguese Wikipedia says about eleven."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to English Wikipedia, Brazil lost about 290 men (200 dead and 90 wounded), and the Argentine-Oriental side 403 (147 dead and 256 wounded). The Portuguese version gives 200 dead and 150 wounded on the Brazilian side.",
        "The Brazilians fell back in order, camped at Passo do Cacequi and then went on to São Lourenço. The Argentine-Oriental army did not pursue them and returned to the Passo do Rosário to collect its baggage. The lack of supplies and the tiredness of the horses help explain this, and Paz and Iriarte criticized Alvear for it.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "For Argentina, it was a tactical victory. For the Brazilians, an inconclusive combat, because Barbacena did not lose his army and Alvear did not invade Rio Grande do Sul. According to historians on both sides, no Brazilian square was broken. Strategically, the war was decided at sea: Brazil kept naval supremacy, Montevideo and Colônia do Sacramento. The British diplomat Ponsonby wrote that the war was, in essence, a naval war.",
        "In 1828, with British mediation, a peace convention, signed in August, recognized the Cisplatine as an independent country, Uruguay. Neither side had managed to win decisively.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The march: a march attributed to Pedro I was captured by the Argentines after the battle and, as the Ituzaingó March, is still played at flag ceremonies in Argentina." },
        { note: "The versions: Argentines and Uruguayans speak of victory, and the Brazilians of an indecisive combat. Argentines and Brazilians also disagree about the outcome of Paz's final charge. The sources we read do not allow us to decide." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  alamo: {
    war: "Texas Revolution (1835–1836)",
    hook: "About 200 men against nearly 2,000, and a defeat that became a battle cry.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the early 1830s, Texas was a province of Mexico inhabited largely by settlers from the United States. Many were slave owners, which Mexican law forbade. In 1835, the Mexican government became centralized, and the settlers rebelled. The Mexican authorities blamed American immigration for the unrest.",
        "The Mexican leader was Antonio López de Santa Anna, who saw himself as the Napoleon of the West. In December 1835, the Tornel decree ordered irregular invaders to be treated as pirates, which meant [[no quarter|sem quartel]] for prisoners. At the end of the year, Santa Anna marched north with about 6,000 men, many of them inexperienced recruits.",
        { timeline: [
          ["December 1835", "The Texans take San Antonio de Béxar. The Tornel decree orders that rebels not be spared."],
          ["23 February 1836", "Santa Anna arrives at Béxar with about 1,500 men, and the siege of the Alamo begins."],
          ["24 February", "Travis writes the 'Victory or Death' letter."],
          ["2 March", "Texas declares independence."],
          ["6 March", "The final assault on the Alamo."],
          ["21 April", "Battle of San Jacinto: Santa Anna is defeated and captured."]
        ]},
        "The Alamo was an old Spanish [[mission|missão]] with thick walls, but poorly supplied and without walls that were easy to defend: the north wall was makeshift and full of holes. In January 1836, fewer than 100 soldiers remained. James Bowie and William Travis shared command after the volunteers elected Bowie."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "William Barret Travis, 26 years old" },
        "An officer in the Texan army, he was left alone in command when Bowie fell ill. He wrote letters asking for help, and the one of 24 February, addressed 'to the people of Texas and all Americans in the world', says he would never surrender or retreat and ends with 'Victory or Death'. In the letter, he speaks of 150 men.",
        { h: "James Bowie and Davy Crockett" },
        "Bowie was an adventurer famous for the knife that bears his name. He was ill, and probably died in bed, although accounts differ. Crockett was a former American congressman from Tennessee who arrived with a group of volunteers. His end is disputed.",
        { h: "Santa Anna and his generals" },
        "Santa Anna insisted on attacking, despite the objections of many of his officers, instead of waiting for the heavy cannons that were on their way. In the assault columns, General Martín Perfecto de Cos (Santa Anna's brother-in-law, defeated at Béxar in December), Duque, Romero and Morales led the columns. General Manuel Fernández Castrillón took over Duque's when he was wounded. The cavalry was under General Joaquín Ramírez y Sesma."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "During the siege, the Mexicans had about 1,500 men on the first day, some 2,000 soon after and about 3,100 on 3 March. In the assault, fewer than 1,800 infantry soldiers in four columns, 500 cavalry around the outside, to prevent escapes, and 400 in reserve at the camp. The garrison started with about 100 men and, with reinforcements, reached 180 to 260. A group of 32 men from Gonzales came in at the start of March.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "The siege lasted 13 days. The Mexican artillery bombarded the walls and crept closer little by little. A red flag, a sign that no quarter would be given, was raised at the church of Béxar, and Travis answered with a cannon shot. The column of Colonel James Fannin, coming from Goliad, turned back after less than a mile.",
        { h: "The assault" },
        { formation: true },
        "The Mexican artillery stopped firing at 10 p.m. on 5 March. Before six in the morning, the troops advanced in silence. The veterans were on the edges of each column to control the recruits, in the middle. Cos led 350 men and 10 ladders to the northwest corner, Duque 400 and 10 ladders to a breach in the north wall, Romero 400 and 6 ladders to the east wall, and Morales 125 and 2 ladders to the low parapet near the church.",
        "The [[grapeshot|metralha]] and rifle fire of the Texans stopped the first two attacks. The recruits, packed into the columns, fired blindly and hit the men in front. Duque was wounded in the thigh and fell from his horse, nearly trampled by his own soldiers.",
        "In the third attack, the columns of Cos and Romero drifted north, where the wall was makeshift and full of holes. Santa Anna, fearing a disaster, sent the reserve there. Juan Amador was one of the first to jump the wall, about 3.5 meters high, and opened the small door from the inside. Others entered through the gun ports on the west side, which had few defenders.",
        "When the Texans abandoned the north wall, the Mexicans also took the south side, killed the gunners and captured the 18-pounder cannon, which the defenders had not disabled. They turned a cannon on the barracks and broke down the doors one by one, with a volley of shots and a hand-to-hand charge. The last to resist were 11 men, operating two 12-pounder cannons in the church. The 18-pounder broke the barricades, and the Mexicans entered after a volley.",
        "Travis was one of the first to die, on the north bastion. By 6:30 the fighting was over, although firing continued for about 15 minutes after the call to retreat. The fight lasted a little over an hour, at most about an hour and a half."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Most witnesses counted between 182 and 257 Texans dead (the official list has 189). The Mexicans are said to have had 400 to 600 dead and wounded, according to most historians, although Santa Anna's first report gave far fewer.",
        "Between five and seven Texans are said to have surrendered, and Santa Anna had them executed. Mexican officers removed the noncombatants, women, children and slaves, and Santa Anna gave each one a blanket and two dollars. Susanna Dickinson and her baby daughter were sent to Gonzales to spread the news. The bodies of the Texans were burned, except that of Gregorio Esparza, which his brother buried.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The siege gave time for the Texas convention to declare independence, on 2 March, and draft a constitution. The news of the fall triggered a mass flight of settlers, the Runaway Scrape, but also drew volunteers to Sam Houston's army. On 21 April, at San Jacinto, the Texans defeated Santa Anna, who was captured, shouting 'Remember the Alamo!'. Texas was a republic until it was annexed to the United States, in 1845.",
        "At Goliad, still in March, Fannin and his men, who had surrendered, were executed on Mexican orders, and that is why the full cry was 'Remember the Alamo! Remember Goliad!'.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The line in the sand: the story of Travis drawing a line with his sword for the men to choose to stay or leave has no support in any source from the time. It appeared decades later, in a third-hand account." },
        { note: "Crockett: some accounts say he surrendered and was executed, and they come mostly from critics of Santa Anna. Others say he died fighting. Historians disagree." },
        { note: "The attack and the motives: some historians think the assault had no military justification, and that Santa Anna's haste cost his soldiers dearly. The Texan cause is usually told as one of freedom, but slavery was among the interests of many settlers, and that is also part of the discussion." },
        { note: "The numbers: the estimates of defenders (from 180 to 260) and of Mexican casualties (from 400 to 600) are disputed, and popular memory, shaped by films and songs, mixes fact and myth." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  zhenjiang: {
    war: "First Opium War (1839–1842)",
    hook: "The city where China lost the thread of Beijing's food supply, and where soldiers killed their own families rather than see defeat.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The [[Opium War|guerra do ópio]] began with trade. British merchants sold opium in China, and in 1839 the imperial commissioner Lin Zexu confiscated and destroyed the opium in Canton. Britain sent a naval and infantry force, and China, with an old-style army and no modern ships, lost point after point.",
        "In the summer of 1842, the British decided to go up the Yangtze River with [[steamships|navio a vapor]] and cut the [[Grand Canal|grande canal]], which crosses the river at Zhenjiang. The rice that fed Beijing passed through there. Whoever controlled that point could strangle the capital without having to reach it.",
        { timeline: [
          ["1839", "Lin Zexu destroys the opium in Canton."],
          ["June 1842", "The British take Wusong and Shanghai."],
          ["21 July 1842", "Battle of Zhenjiang."],
          ["29 August 1842", "Signing of the Treaty of Nanjing."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hugh Gough, 62 years old" },
        "An Irishman, he commanded the British land forces. One of his brigade commanders was Colin Campbell, the future field marshal. Lord Saltoun commanded the first brigade.",
        { h: "Hailing" },
        "The Manchu commander of the Zhenjiang garrison. The Manchus were a people who had conquered China in 1644, and their soldiers, the [[Eight Banners|oito bandeiras]], formed the hereditary elite of the Qing army. According to Wikipedia, Hailing killed himself after the battle, along with his family, although another passage of the same entry has him dying in combat. ",
        { h: "The emperor" },
        "Emperor Daoguang, in Beijing, saw the point at which the city was taken as the limit. It was after this that he agreed to negotiate peace."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British had 6,907 men, in four brigades, plus the navy. The defenders were soldiers of the [[Eight Banners|oito bandeiras]], Manchus and Mongols, about 1,583 in the city, and some 2,700 of the [[Green Standard Army|estandarte verde]], mostly Han Chinese, in the southwest suburb. Wikipedia estimates the Chinese total at between 3,000 and 4,000. A British source of the time says only 1,200 to 1,500 men appeared before the attack.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Zhenjiang was a walled city in Jiangsu, on the south bank of the Yangtze, at the point where the river crosses the Grand Canal. Inside it lay an inner city, the Tatar city, where the Manchu families lived. There were three fortified camps outside the walls.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Before dawn on 21 July, the first brigade landed and took the hills near Jinshan. At 7 a.m., the third brigade landed at Mount Beigu and attacked the north gate, with support from the ships' artillery. The soldiers of the Eight Banners wounded two British captains, but their battery was destroyed after more than an hour, and the fight moved to the walls.",
        "At 8 a.m., the British attacked the Green Standard Army outside the city. These soldiers, tired and poorly fed, were defeated. Their commander, seeing fire inside the city, thought it had already fallen and ordered a retreat. Many deserted.",
        "At the west gate, the main attack, the second brigade fired from nearby houses, while marines tried to get there by the canal. The garrison repelled them in ten minutes. About 200 marines reinforced the attack and took buildings in street fighting. At noon, engineers blew open the west gate with gunpowder, and the third brigade, which had come from the north gate, entered. The defense collapsed.",
        "The hardest part was the Tatar city. The Manchus resisted with fire from inside the houses, and Gough ordered an immediate attack. The Manchus were pushed back everywhere, but the British, exhausted by the heat, could not take advantage. In that pause, according to British accounts, many Manchus killed their wives and children and then killed themselves."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Wikipedia gives 37 British dead, 128 wounded and 3 missing, and about 1,000 Chinese dead or wounded. An account of the time speaks of 40 dead and 130 wounded among the British and adds that several other soldiers died of heat and exhaustion that day.",
        "The city fell, and the canal was blocked. Emperor Daoguang decided to ask for peace. On 29 August 1842, aboard the British ship Cornwallis, at Nanjing, the [[Treaty of Nanjing|tratado de nanquim]] was signed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The treaty handed Hong Kong to the British, opened five ports to trade (Canton, Xiamen, Fuzhou, Ningbo and Shanghai) and ended the monopoly of the Cohong merchants in Canton. China also agreed to pay 21 million silver dollars: 6 million for the confiscated opium, 3 for merchants' debts and 12 for the costs of the war, minus whatever the British forces had already received as ransom from Chinese cities after 1 August 1841. The Chinese call this treaty and the ones that followed unequal treaties, and the period that began there the 'century of humiliation'.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The mass suicide: the accounts of Manchus killing their own families and themselves come mostly from British sources. This kind of ending was seen as a way of preserving honor, but we have no way to verify numbers." },
        { note: "The fate of Hailing: Wikipedia gives two versions, that he and his family killed themselves and that he died in combat." },
        { note: "The name: the city appears in British sources of the time as Chinkiang or Chinkiang-foo." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

